==English==
{{was wotd|2018|January|2}}

===Etymology===
[[File:CumulonimbusFlorida.jpg|thumb|[[cumulonimbus|Cumulonimbus]] [[cloud#Noun|clouds]] forming over the [[Gulf of Mexico]], seen from [[Florida]], USA]]

The origin of sense 1 (“a state of bliss”) is uncertain; the following etymologies have been suggested:

* The first edition of the ''{{w|International Cloud Atlas}}'' (1896),<ref>{{cite-book|author=International Meterological Committee|authorlink=International Meteorological Organization|author2=H[ugo Hildebrand] Hildebrandsson|authorlink2=Hugo Hildebrand Hildebrandsson|author3=A[lbert] Riggenbach|authorlink3=Albert Riggenbach|author4=L[éon] Teisserenc de Bort|authorlink4=Léon Teisserenc de Bort|title=[[w:International Cloud Atlas|Atlas international des nuages = International Cloud-atlas = Internationaler Wolken-Atlas]]|location=Paris|publisher=Gauthier-Villars|year=1896|oclc=715068869}}.</ref> which defined ten types of cloud, described the ninth type as the [[cumulonimbus]] which rises to 10 km (6.2 miles), the highest a cloud can be.<ref name="Etymonline">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|url=https://www.etymonline.com/word/cloud%20nine|accessdate=22 December 2017}}</ref>
* In traditional [[Han Chinese]] mythology the sky has nine layers. The ninth cloud ({{m|zh|九霄|tr=jiǔxiāo}}: {{m|zh|九|tr=jiǔ||nine}} + {{m|zh|霄|tr=xiāo||sky; clouds, mist; night}}) is the highest part of the sky or, in [[Taoism]], [[heaven]] where the gods live.

Compare {{m|en|cloud seven||state of complete happiness or euphoria}},<ref>{{cite-book|author=Harold Wentworth and {{w|Stuart Berg Flexner}}, compilers and editors|title=Dictionary of American Slang|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Thomas T. Crowell|year=1960|oclc=803362919}}.</ref> which may have originated from confusion of ''cloud nine'' with {{m|en|seventh heaven}}.<ref name="Etymonline"/>

Sense 2 (“a state of fantastic or impractical dreaming or thinking”) may be due to a confusion between sense 1 and the phrase {{m|en|head in the clouds}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈklaʊd ˈnaɪn/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} ''Often in the phrase'' '''{{l|en|on cloud nine}}''': a [[state#Noun|state]] of [[bliss]], [[elation]] or [[happiness]].
#: {{ux|en|He was on '''cloud nine''' for days after she agreed to marry him.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Watchman Nee|authorlink=Watchman Nee|chapter=Living by Faith|title=The Salvation of the Soul: Translated from the Chinese|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Christian Fellowship Publishers|year=1978|page=107|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=kEC9BQAAQBAJ&pg=PA107|isbn=978-0-935008-31-9|passage=In your zeal you may not feel you are undertaking a thing for your own sake but think you are doing so for God. Nevertheless, you should remember that at the time of great excitement as though you were on '''cloud nine''', you may perhaps be most fleshly!}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Shirlee Emmons|authorlink=Shirlee Emmons|author2=Alma Thomas|chapter=Postperformance Management|chapterurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=NQfxYpPwLhoC&pg=PT395|title=Power Performance for Singers: Transcending the Barriers|location=New York, N.Y.; Oxford|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=1998|isbn=978-0-19-511224-5|passage=Following a highly successful tour of Italy in July/August 1993, with recital and orchestral appearances and an especially inspiring last performance, I returned to New York on '''cloud nine''' and full of euphoria.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Thumbiko Shumba|chapter=1|title=Worklife|location=Pittsburgh, Pa.|publisher=RoseDog Books|year=2010|page=1|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=DszzRX6M4G8C&pg=PA1|isbn=978-1-4349-4578-5|passage=On the first day at the university I was on '''cloud nine''' that I had begun my intellectual life. I was happy to join the cream of creams.}}
# {{lb|en|possibly|nonstandard}} A state of [[fantastic]] or [[impractical]] [[dreaming#Noun|dreaming]] or [[thinking#Noun|thinking]].

